.TH XD\-WUAUCPL 1 2026-10-18 XeonDesktop "XeonDesktop Manual"
.SH NAME
xd\-wuaucpl \- Automatic Updates
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY xd\-wuaucpl
.OP \-\-page n
.YS
.SY xd\-wuaucpl
.B \-\-check
.YS
.SY xd\-wuaucpl
.B \-\-history
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xd\-wuaucpl
is Windows XP's Automatic Updates (Control Panel), with the updates
themselves, which XP left to the Windows Update web site:
.TP
.B Automatic Updates
Whether to download updates and let you choose when to install them,
only tell you about them, or not check at all; and when to check (every
day, or one day of the week, at an hour).
Updates are never installed until you click Install, and never downloaded
while NetworkManager says the connection is metered.
.TP
.B Software Updates
The distribution's updates (through PackageKit) and those of Flatpak apps
and runtimes (the user's installation and the system ones), with their
importance, where they come from, what they fix and whether a restart is
needed.
Check the ones to install, then click Install; PackageKit and Flatpak ask
for an administrator's password where needed.
Check for Updates refreshes the lists first.
When an install fails, the error box says why in a sentence or two, lists
the packages or files involved, and Details shows everything the package
manager reported.
On Arch Linux (PackageKit's alpm backend), which upgrades the whole system
or nothing, Install asks before installing only some of the updates.
View Installation History lists what was installed, downloaded or failed,
and why.
.TP
.B Firmware
The devices fwupd knows, their firmware versions and the updates the Linux
Vendor Firmware Service has for them.
Update downloads one, checks it against its checksum and gives it to
fwupd to install, which asks for an administrator's password.
.TP
.B Device Security
fwupd's Host Security ID (HSI) and the protections it is made of
(Secure Boot, the TPM, the IOMMU, the firmware's write protection, ...),
level by level.
.PP
.BR xd\-shell (1)
checks on the schedule chosen (running
.BR "xd\-wuaucpl \-\-check" ),
and once a check finds updates shows an icon in the notification area
and XP's balloon; a click on either opens Software Updates.
When the check fails (a download, say), its balloon opens the
installation history, which says why.
A check missed while the computer was off runs a few minutes after the
next log on.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-page " n"
Open on the
.IR n th
tab, from 0 (Automatic Updates).
.TP
.B \-\-check
Without a window: refresh PackageKit's lists without asking for a
password, count the updates waiting (packages, Flatpaks and firmware) and,
in the download mode and off metered connections, download the software
updates without installing them (PackageKit's only-download, Flatpak's
no-deploy).
What it found goes to the state file below, and its failures to the
installation history and standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-history
Open Software Updates with the installation history over it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xeondesktop/desktop.ini
.BR [Updates] :
.BR Mode " (" download ", " notify " or " off ),
.BR Day " (" daily ", " sunday " ... " saturday )
and
.BR Hour " (0 to 23)."
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/xeondesktop/updates.ini
What the last check found, which the shell's icon shows.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/xeondesktop/update-history.ini
The installation history: the last 100 installs, and the scheduled
check's failures, with what went wrong.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xd\-shell (1),
.BR xd\-appwiz (1),
.BR xd\-control (1),
.BR pkcon (1),
.BR flatpak (1),
.BR fwupdmgr (1)
